Day 4/45 of my #45DaysofDevOps challenge - Hands-on Linux Troubleshooting!
Yesterday I studied the theory of Linux Process Management. Today, I put that knowledge into practice inside a live Linux terminal, focusing on File Permissions and Log Inspection.
What I implemented today:
Task 1: Fixing log Permissions
Used chmod 755 /var/log/auth.log to change restricted permissions. This ensures the file is readable and executable by all users, while keeping write access restricted to the owner. Verified the changes using ls -1.
Task 2: Extracting IP Addresses with Grep
Used grep -i -nE "192\. 168 | 10\ .0" /var/log/syslog /var/log/auth.log to scan system logs for specific private IP ranges. This helps identify traffic patterns and potential security anomalies in real-time.
Why it matters:
In production, log files are the black box of a server. If a service crashes or a container gets compromised, checking logs is the first step. Knowing how to securely manage file permissions and efficiently filter massive log files with grep is a daily requirement for any DeVops or Cloud Engineer.
